# Lesson 6, Mike's Exercise 2, turned so the student (South) is on lead.
title: Your Exercise 2: fourth from your longest and strongest
id: L06-03
type: exercise
lesson: 6
concepts: 6.5, 6.4
student: S
board: 3

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  J96    T952   Q3      AT54
  E  K82    AKJ4   K84     J93
  S  T74    86     AJ952   K87
  W  AQ53   Q73    T76     Q62

auction:
  S Pass   = Not enough to open
  W Pass   = Not enough to open
  N Pass   = Not enough to open
  E 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
  S Pass   = Nothing to say
  W 2C     = Stayman: do you have a 4-card major?
  N Pass   = Nothing to say
  E 2H     = Four hearts
  S Pass   = Nothing to say
  W 3NT    = Game values, four spades, not four hearts
  N Pass   = Nothing to say
  E Pass   = No four spades: 3NT it is
  S Pass   = Nothing to add

auction recap:
  East opened 1NT. West's Stayman 2♣ found four hearts with East, but West's
  3NT said "not hearts for me; I have four spades." East has no four spades,
  so the contract is 3NT. East is declarer, and so you, South, will lead to
  the first trick.

lead planning:
  declarer: 15-17 = East's 1NT showed 15 to 17, and 2♥ showed four hearts.
  dummy: 10-12 = West passed at first, so under 13, then bid game: 10 to 12, with four spades.
  partner: 3-7 = 40, minus your 8, East's 15 to 17 and West's 10 to 12, leaves 3 to 7.
  remind: The opponents have shown both majors. Which suits has nobody bid?
  remind: At no-trump, your longest and strongest suit is where your extra tricks will come from.

play:
  1   S D5*  W D6  N DQ  E DK
      D5 key: Fourth from your longest and strongest. Leading away from your ace is fine against no-trump: you're starting to set up the suit.
      D5 not DA: Right suit! But the ace is your way back in later, once the suit is set up. Lead low and keep the ace.
      D5 not D: Right suit! The usual card is the fourth from the top: count down A, J, 9, 5.
      D5 not S: Dummy has four spades. Which suit is your longest and strongest?
      D5 not H: Declarer has four hearts. Which suit is your longest and strongest?
      D5 not C: Clubs are a fair unbid suit, but diamonds are longer and stronger.
      note: Partner's queen forces out declarer's king.
  2   E H4   S H6  W HQ  N H2
      note: Declarer counts nine tricks on top: four spades (if they split 3-3), four hearts and the ♦K. She starts with the hearts, dummy's short side first.
  3   W H3   N H5  E HA  S H8
  4   E HK   S C7  W H7  N H9
      note: You have no hearts left, so you throw a small club and keep your diamonds.
  5   E HJ   S C8  W C2  N HT
      note: The fourth heart is good too.
  6   E SK   S S4  W S3  N S6
      note: Now spades, starting with the king from the short side.
  7   E S2   S S7  W SA  N S9
  8   W SQ   N SJ  E S8  S ST
  9   W S5   N C4  E C3  S D2
      note: The spades split 3-3, so dummy's ♠5 is the ninth trick.
  10  W DT   N D3  E D4  S DJ
      note: Declarer has made the contract and gives up the rest.
  11  S DA   W D7  N C5  E D8
  12  S D9   W C6  N CT  E C9
  13  S CK   W CQ  N CA  E CJ

play recap:
  The ♦5 was the right start: your longest and strongest suit, attacking from
  the very first trick. Partner's ♦Q forced out declarer's king, and the rest
  of your diamonds were ready to run. This time declarer won the race: she had
  nine tricks without needing to set anything up, so no lead could beat 3NT.
  When declarer has to give up the lead even once, a head start like this is
  what beats the contract.
